  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 AM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nCurrent-Mon...Another cool start to the morning with temperatures \nbeginning in the 40s/50s. At least one more dry day today, with \nmoisture gradually returning into Mon and an increase in PoP chances \nesp along the Space/Treasure coasts. Onshore flow strengthens on Mon \nwith breezy/gusty conditions nearly areawide. An upper level \ndisturbance moves into the Deep South on Mon and across the Florida \npeninsula Mon night with ISOLD-SCT showers forecast. Slightly below \nnormal temperatures today/Mon with maxes in the L-M70s, except few \nU70s across the Treasure Coast. With the addition of moisture and \nstrengthening of onshore flow our min temps overnight into Mon \nmorning will recover into the M-U50s over the interior and L60s near \nthe coast, except M60s along the St. Lucie/Martin coasts. For Mon \nnight/Tue morning our lows will only fall into the U50s N/W of I-4 \nwith 60s most everywhere else.\n\nTue-Sat...Surface high pressure encompasses much of the eastern \nCONUS and combined with lower pressure across the western Atlc will \nproduce a tight pressure gradient through much of this period. This \nwill allow for a continuation of breezy to potentially windy onshore \n(gusty) winds each day remaining a bit elevated over the interior \nand breezy/gusty along the coast each night. An unsettled (troughy) \npattern will continue aloft across the Florida peninsula with ample \nmoisture in place. This set-up could allow for heavy rainfall (esp \nalong the coast) at least Tue-Thu. Presently, the Weather Prediction \nCenter has outlooked eastern FL under a Marginal threat for \nExcessive Rainfall these three days. Low confidence in exactly where \nheavy bands may set up, but we do not anticipate as much rainfall as \nwe saw a couple of weeks ago. Much will depend on the \nstrength/position of a low/trough forecast to develop near/east of \nthe Bahamas early/mid week. This feature will gradually track \nnortheast away from eastern FL, but additional impacts will include \npoor/hazardous boating conditions and building seas creating rough, \npounding surf along the coast with minor beach erosion likely, as \nwell as strong rip currents. Rain chances increase areawide (Tue-\nFri) - highest along the coast. A low chance for lightning storms \nwill also be forecast. By Wed, temperatures for highs will be near \nto slightly above climo. Low temperatures will be above normal for \nmuch of this period.",
